WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.830
Alaska, 1941 to 2004.

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:07.670
The front of Muir Glacier
pulls back 12 kilometres.

3
00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:10.850
Yet all that time, its
ice kept moving downhill,

4
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.650
towards the sea.

5
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:20.790
Worldwide, glaciers lost about 273
gigatonnes of ice a year from 2000 to 2023.

6
00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.630
So how can a glacier move
forward and still retreat?

7
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.750
Does it flow uphill at times?

8
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.610
In this mission, you'll
treat a glacier as a system,

9
00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:32.930
with inputs, a store and outputs.

10
00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:36.670
Then you'll see how ice
moves, by sliding or creeping,

11
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.910
and how it plucks and
scrapes the rock beneath.

12
00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:45.130
Your mission: explain how Muir Glacier
could retreat 12 kilometres while its ice

13
00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:46.760
flowed towards the sea.
